@meeting talk.ferreira
// SW2, 0.92.6 item 13: Day 46, Thursday 19:00-19:45, a chief's talk in the auditorium seats. Lucia Ferreira, Comms
// Chief: "Messages that came home late". She never reads a message: the talk is about delays, never about contents.
title: Messages that came home late
from: Lucia Ferreira
gist: Ferreira's talk on the relay, the dust, and the birthday message that arrived a year late.
== lines
ferreira: Hello, hello! Sit anywhere. Chef sent biscuits, so be nice to the person passing them.   [gesture]
ferreira: Every message home goes through my office. I never read them. I do count them.   [laugh]
ferreira: Out past the Line the relay's slow. A message can wait days for a gap in the dust.
ferreira: The latest one I ever delivered was a birthday message. Nearly a year late.
ferreira: Three hundred and sixty-four days. It landed on the next birthday. Right on time.   [laugh]
ferreira: Late isn't lost. Somebody always keeps a message safe until it can go. That's us.   [nod]
ferreira: So write home, even when the relay's slow. Now, questions! I love questions.
? choice
* Where does a message wait?   rel:ferreira+2 flavour:practical mem:talk.ferreira.asked
  ferreira: In the relay queue, safe as a letter in a drawer. When a gap opens, off it goes.   [nod]
* Do you ever peek? Even once?   rel:ferreira+2 flavour:cheer mem:talk.ferreira.asked
  ferreira: Never, ever. I read the addresses. That's quite enough gossip for anyone.   [laugh]
* (Just listen)
  ferreira: No questions? Then go and write to someone. I'll see it gets there.
ferreira: Thank you all. Bye for now, and write home.
